---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../lib/siteUrl';
import FeatureVisual from '../../components/FeatureVisual.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Code Export"
  description="Inspect design-to-code output in Varve's shared Code panel from any workspace, with format-specific support notes and audit findings."
>
  <div class="container-custom">
    <article class="feature-page">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={sitePath('/features')}>Features</a> / Code Export
      </nav>
      <h1 class="site-page-title">Code export, wherever you work</h1>
      <p class="feature-intro">
        The shared Code panel brings generated output and design checks into any workspace. Open it
        without changing the active task or leaving the selected artwork.
      </p>

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>Compare output and understand limits</h2>
        <p>
          Inspect supported scene content as SVG, HTML, React, Tailwind, CSS Modules, Flutter, or
          SwiftUI. Format-specific warnings identify content that needs a fallback or manual
          adjustment, and content the target cannot represent is rasterized from the same render
          pipeline the canvas uses — never emitted as a placeholder. Export output is generated
          from the document; it is not an arbitrary project source-code editor.
        </p>
        <p>
          Text survives the trip as text: paragraph structure, styled runs, and right-to-left
          direction carry into semantic HTML, styled spans, Flutter rich text, and SwiftUI styled
          concatenation. SVG text is placed from the same measured layout the canvas renders
          with, so line breaks match what you see. Effects that CSS can express natively — inset
          shadows, conic gradients, layered fills, blur — are emitted as real CSS; the rest ships
          as a pre-rendered asset inside the bundle.
        </p>
        <p>
          <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>8</kbd> shows the Code panel in the current
          workspace. The existing <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>J</kbd> shortcut toggles
          that same panel. On macOS, use Command in place of Ctrl. The panel also includes design
          audit and readiness views where applicable.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>Preview the real output, sandboxed</h2>
        <p>
          SVG and HTML previews render the generated bytes inside a fully locked-down iframe —
          opaque origin, no scripts, no access to the editor or your documents — at phone, tablet,
          or full width. Targets that need a build runtime say so instead of showing a transformed
          stand-in: a preview that is not the deliverable would teach you the wrong thing.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>Bundles that travel</h2>
        <p>
          Multi-file exports are self-contained: code files, content-addressed asset files, and a
          manifest recording exactly what the generator produced and at which content hashes.
          Regeneration planning uses that manifest to tell unchanged generated files apart from
          files you have edited — edited files are flagged as conflicts, handwritten files are
          never touched, and nothing is deleted without you.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>Verified, honestly</h2>
        <p>
          The web profile is verified against real pinned toolchains: the exported bundle is
          type-checked and production-built with React, Vite, and Tailwind's actual compiler in a
          clean consumer project. Flutter and SwiftUI output is emitted and reviewed but has not
          been compiled by their SDKs on every platform — where that verification has not run, we
          say so rather than claim it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <FeatureVisual
        scene="workspace-shared-workflows"
        caption="The Code panel is a shared surface — here it is open beside Logo tools in Design."
      />

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>One shared surface</h2>
        <p>
          Code is a panel and export workflow rather than a separate workspace. It remains available
          alongside Design, Print, Draw, Photo, Motion, and Email. See
          <a href={sitePath('/features/export')}>Export &amp; Code Generation</a> for file-format
          details and <a href={sitePath('/features/workspaces')}>all six workspaces</a>.
        </p>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .feature-page { max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 3rem 0; }
  .breadcrumb { font-size: var(--type-interface-caption-size); margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
  .breadcrumb a { color: var(--text-secondary); }
  .feature-intro { font-size: var(--type-content-lead-size); color: var(--text-secondary); margin: 1rem 0 3rem; line-height: 1.7; }
  .feature-section { margin: 0 0 3rem; }
  .feature-section h2 { font-size: var(--type-interface-heading-size); margin-bottom: 1rem; color: var(--text-primary); }
  .feature-section p { color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.7; }
</style>
